        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Code of Hammurabi</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1753 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>, sixth king of the Amorite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty of Babylon</a>, in whose name the text was purportedly written near the end of his reign; the scribes and the draughtsman of the relief, working in a tradition of earlier Sumerian and Akkadian law collections issued by rulers.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">About 4,130 lines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> in the Old Babylonian dialect of Akkadian: a 300-line prologue and a 500-line epilogue in poetic style around casuistic “if ... then” laws; the primary copy is a basalt stele 2.25 m tall, topped by a relief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> with the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">The stele is on the second floor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, in Room 227 of the Richelieu wing; over 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the laws come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borsippa">Borsippa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> and other sites; replicas stand at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, the Peace Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> and the Pergamon Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-talionis">lex talionis</a>, “an eye for an eye”; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>'s aim “to prevent the strong from oppressing the weak”; penalties that differ by class and gender; laws on physicians, builders and prices; the curses of the epilogue; the debate over whether it is a true code; comparison with the Covenant Code of Exodus</span></div>
