    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Composed near the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>'s reign, the text sets about 4,130 lines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> on a basalt stele: a poetic prologue and epilogue around laws phrased as “if ... then” cases on criminal, family, property and commercial matters.</li><li>Its laws rest on principles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-talionis">lex talionis</a>, “an eye for an eye”, and treat people unequally by class and gender, distinguishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awilum">awīlum</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mushkenum">muškēnum</a> and the slave.</li><li>Taken as plunder to Susa and found there in 1901, the stele is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>; scholars still debate whether the text was enforced legislation, a record of past cases or a work of scholarship.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The epilogue hopes that “any wronged man who has a lawsuit” may have the laws of the stele read aloud to him and know his rights; its curses on any future defacer run to 281 lines.</p></aside>
    </section>

